======================================================================
1. WHAT IS ABSTRACTION?
----------------------------------------------------------------------

Interview Answer

Abstraction is one of the four pillars of OOP.

It is the process of hiding implementation details and exposing
only the essential functionality to the user.

In Java, abstraction is mainly achieved using:

1. Abstract classes
2. Interfaces

Example:

When we use an ATM, we see options such as:

    Withdraw
    Deposit
    Check Balance

But we don't need to know the internal implementation of how
the banking transaction is processed.

This is abstraction.


======================================================================
2. WHY IS ABSTRACTION NEEDED?
----------------------------------------------------------------------

Abstraction is needed to:

• Hide unnecessary implementation details
• Reduce complexity
• Expose only essential functionality
• Improve maintainability
• Provide loose coupling
• Allow implementation to change without affecting users

Example:

User
  |
  v
ATM Interface
  |
  v
Hidden Banking Implementation


======================================================================
3. WHAT IS AN ABSTRACT CLASS?
----------------------------------------------------------------------

Interview Answer

An abstract class is a class declared using the `abstract`
keyword that cannot be instantiated directly.

It can contain:

• Abstract methods
• Concrete methods
• Variables
• Constructors

Example:

abstract class Animal {

    abstract void sound();

    void eat() {
        System.out.println("Animal eats");
    }
}

class Dog extends Animal {

    @Override
    void sound() {
        System.out.println("Dog barks");
    }
}

Here:

Animal → Abstract class
sound() → Abstract method
eat() → Concrete method
Dog → Child class


======================================================================
4. WHEN SHOULD YOU USE AN ABSTRACT CLASS?
----------------------------------------------------------------------

Use an abstract class when:

• Multiple related classes share common behavior/state.
• Some functionality can be implemented in the parent.
• Some behavior must be implemented by child classes.

Example:

abstract class Animal {

    void eat() {
        System.out.println("Eating");
    }

    abstract void sound();
}

Dog and Cat can inherit `eat()` but provide their own
implementation of `sound()`.

Memory Trick:

Abstract Class

    Common functionality
           +
    Child-specific implementation


======================================================================
5. WHAT IS AN INTERFACE?
----------------------------------------------------------------------

Interview Answer

An interface in Java is a blueprint or contract that defines
behavior that implementing classes must follow.

A class implements an interface using the `implements` keyword.

Example:

interface Animal {

    void sound();
}

class Dog implements Animal {

    @Override
    public void sound() {
        System.out.println("Dog barks");
    }
}

Here:

Animal → Interface
sound() → Contract
Dog → Implementing class


======================================================================
6. WHEN SHOULD YOU USE AN INTERFACE?
----------------------------------------------------------------------

Use an interface when you want to:

• Define a common contract
• Achieve abstraction
• Achieve loose coupling
• Allow different classes to follow the same contract
• Support multiple inheritance of type

Example:

interface Payment {
    void pay();
}

class CreditCard implements Payment {
    public void pay() {
        System.out.println("Paid using Credit Card");
    }
}

class UPI implements Payment {
    public void pay() {
        System.out.println("Paid using UPI");
    }
}

Both classes follow the same contract but have different
implementations.


======================================================================
7. ABSTRACT CLASS vs INTERFACE
----------------------------------------------------------------------

+----------------------+-----------------------------+
| Abstract Class       | Interface                   |
+----------------------+-----------------------------+
| Declared with        | Declared with               |
| `abstract`           | `interface`                 |
+----------------------+-----------------------------+
| Extended using       | Implemented using           |
| `extends`            | `implements`                |
+----------------------+-----------------------------+
| Can contain abstract | Can contain abstract,       |
| and concrete methods | default and static methods  |
+----------------------+-----------------------------+
| Can have instance    | Does not normally represent |
| state/variables      | object state                |
+----------------------+-----------------------------+
| Can have constructor | Cannot have constructors    |
+----------------------+-----------------------------+
| Class can extend     | A class can implement       |
| only one class       | multiple interfaces         |
+----------------------+-----------------------------+

IMPORTANT:

Abstract class → shared state + shared implementation

Interface → contract + abstraction


======================================================================
8. CAN AN ABSTRACT CLASS CONTAIN CONSTRUCTORS?
----------------------------------------------------------------------

YES.

An abstract class can contain constructors even though we cannot
create its object directly.

Why?

When an object of a concrete child class is created, the
constructor of the parent abstract class is executed as part of
the object creation process.

Example:

abstract class Animal {

    Animal() {
        System.out.println("Animal constructor");
    }
}

class Dog extends Animal {

    Dog() {
        System.out.println("Dog constructor");
    }
}

public class Main {

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Dog d = new Dog();
    }
}

Output:

Animal constructor
Dog constructor


IMPORTANT:

Cannot instantiate abstract class

    ≠

Cannot have constructor


======================================================================
9. CAN AN INTERFACE HAVE METHODS WITH IMPLEMENTATION?
----------------------------------------------------------------------

YES.

This is an important Java interview trap.

Since Java 8:

1. default methods
2. static methods

can have implementations inside interfaces.

Since Java 9:

3. private methods

can also have implementations.

Example:

interface Vehicle {

    void start();

    default void stop() {
        System.out.println("Vehicle stopped");
    }

    static void info() {
        System.out.println("Vehicle interface");
    }
}

Important:

Normal abstract method:

void start();

does not contain implementation.

But:

default void stop() {
    System.out.println("Vehicle stopped");
}

contains implementation.


======================================================================
ABSTRACTION vs ENCAPSULATION
----------------------------------------------------------------------

VERY IMPORTANT INTERVIEW TRAP


ABSTRACTION

Focus:

"What should be exposed?"

Hides:

Implementation details

Achieved mainly using:

• Abstract class
• Interface


ENCAPSULATION

Focus:

"How should data be protected?"

Hides:

Direct access to data

Achieved using:

• private fields
• getters
• setters


Memory Trick:

Abstraction
    ↓
Hide IMPLEMENTATION

Encapsulation
    ↓
Protect DATA


======================================================================
COMMON INTERVIEW TRAPS
----------------------------------------------------------------------

❌ "Abstract class can only contain abstract methods."

✅ Abstract class can contain both abstract and concrete methods.


❌ "Abstract class cannot have a constructor."

✅ Abstract class can have constructors.


❌ "Interface can contain only abstract methods."

✅ Modern Java interfaces can contain:

• abstract methods
• default methods
• static methods
• private methods


❌ "Abstract class is inherited using abstract keyword."

✅ Abstract class is inherited using `extends`.

Example:

class Dog extends Animal


❌ "Interface is inherited using implements."

Technically:

A class IMPLEMENTS an interface.

Example:

class Dog implements Animal


======================================================================
QUICK REVISION
----------------------------------------------------------------------

ABSTRACTION
    ↓
Hide implementation
Show essential functionality
    ↓
Abstract Class / Interface


ABSTRACT CLASS
    ↓
Cannot instantiate directly
    ↓
Abstract + Concrete methods
    ↓
Can have constructors
    ↓
Child uses extends


INTERFACE
    ↓
Contract / Blueprint
    ↓
Class uses implements
    ↓
Supports multiple interfaces
    ↓
Can have abstract/default/static/private methods


ABSTRACT CLASS vs INTERFACE

Abstract Class
    → Shared state + implementation

Interface
    → Contract + abstraction


======================================================================
ONE-MINUTE INTERVIEW REVISION
----------------------------------------------------------------------

Q: What is abstraction?
A: Hiding implementation details and exposing essential
   functionality.

Q: How is abstraction achieved?
A: Mainly through abstract classes and interfaces.

Q: What is an abstract class?
A: A class declared with abstract that cannot be instantiated
   directly and can contain abstract and concrete methods.

Q: When use abstract class?
A: When related classes share common state/behavior but need
   different implementations for some methods.

Q: What is an interface?
A: A contract that implementing classes must follow.

Q: When use interface?
A: For abstraction, loose coupling, common contracts, and
   multiple inheritance of type.

Q: Can abstract class have constructor?
A: Yes. It initializes the parent portion of a child object.

Q: Can interface have implemented methods?
A: Yes. Since Java 8 default/static methods and since Java 9
   private methods can have implementations.


