Questions Covered

✓ Q1. What is a constructor in Java?
✓ Q2. What are the different types of constructors?
✓ Q3. What is the difference between a constructor and a method?


======================================================================
1. WHAT IS A CONSTRUCTOR?
----------------------------------------------------------------------

Interview Answer

A constructor is a special member of a class used to initialize
objects.

It has:

• Same name as the class
• No return type, not even void
• Automatically invoked during object creation
• Used primarily for object initialization

Example:

class Student {

    Student() {
        System.out.println("Constructor called");
    }
}

Student s = new Student();

When `new Student()` creates the object, the constructor is invoked.


======================================================================
2. IMPORTANT CONSTRUCTOR POINTS
----------------------------------------------------------------------

✔ Constructor name must match the class name.

✔ Constructor has NO return type.

❌ This is NOT a constructor:

void Student() {
}

Because `void` makes it a method.

✔ Constructor is called during object creation.

✔ A constructor is NOT technically a method.

✔ A constructor is called once for EACH object creation.

Example:

Student s1 = new Student();
Student s2 = new Student();

The constructor executes once for `s1` and once for `s2`.


======================================================================
3. TYPES OF CONSTRUCTORS
----------------------------------------------------------------------

Commonly discussed types:

1. No-Argument Constructor
2. Parameterized Constructor
3. Default Constructor


----------------------------------------------------------------------
3.1 NO-ARGUMENT CONSTRUCTOR
----------------------------------------------------------------------

A constructor that accepts no parameters.

Example:

class Student {

    Student() {
        System.out.println("Student created");
    }
}


----------------------------------------------------------------------
3.2 PARAMETERIZED CONSTRUCTOR
----------------------------------------------------------------------

A constructor that accepts parameters to initialize an object
with specific values.

Example:

class Student {

    String name;
    int age;

    Student(String name, int age) {
        this.name = name;
        this.age = age;
    }
}

Usage:

Student s = new Student("Vishal", 21);


----------------------------------------------------------------------
3.3 DEFAULT CONSTRUCTOR
----------------------------------------------------------------------

If the programmer does not declare ANY constructor, the compiler
automatically provides a default constructor.

Example:

class Student {

    String name;
}

Conceptually, the compiler provides something like:

Student() {
    super();
}


======================================================================
4. DEFAULT vs NO-ARGUMENT CONSTRUCTOR
----------------------------------------------------------------------

VERY IMPORTANT INTERVIEW TRAP


DEFAULT CONSTRUCTOR

→ Automatically provided by the compiler
→ Provided only when no constructor is declared


NO-ARGUMENT CONSTRUCTOR

→ Any constructor having zero parameters
→ Can be written by the programmer
→ Can also be compiler-provided


Example:

class Student {

    Student() {
        // Programmer-defined no-argument constructor
    }
}

This is a:

✓ No-argument constructor

But it is NOT the compiler-generated default constructor.


======================================================================
5. CONSTRUCTOR vs METHOD
----------------------------------------------------------------------

+---------------------+------------------------------+
| Constructor         | Method                       |
+---------------------+------------------------------+
| Initializes object  | Performs an operation        |
| Same name as class  | Has its own name             |
| No return type      | Has return type or void      |
| Called during       | Called explicitly            |
| object creation     |                              |
| Called once per     | Can be called multiple times |
| object creation     |                              |
| Not inherited       | Can be inherited              |
| Cannot be overridden| Can be overridden             |
+---------------------+------------------------------+


======================================================================
6. CONSTRUCTOR EXAMPLE
----------------------------------------------------------------------

class Student {

    String name;
    int age;

    Student(String name, int age) {

        this.name = name;
        this.age = age;
    }

    void display() {

        System.out.println(name);
        System.out.println(age);
    }
}

public class Main {

    public static void main(String[] args) {

        Student s = new Student("Vishal", 21);

        s.display();
    }
}

Here:

Student(...) → Constructor

display() → Method


======================================================================
7. CONSTRUCTOR OVERLOADING
----------------------------------------------------------------------

A class can have multiple constructors with different parameter
lists.

Example:

class Student {

    Student() {
    }

    Student(String name) {
    }

    Student(String name, int age) {
    }
}

This is called:

Constructor Overloading


IMPORTANT:

Constructor overloading is different from method overloading.

Constructor:

Student()
Student(String)
Student(String, int)

Method:

display()
display(String)
display(String, int)


======================================================================
8. COMMON INTERVIEW TRAPS
----------------------------------------------------------------------

❌ "Constructor is a special type of method."

Better answer:

✅ "Constructor is a special member of a class used to initialize
objects. Technically, it is not a method."


--------------------------------------------------

❌ "Constructor is called only once."

✅ "Constructor is called once for each object creation."


--------------------------------------------------

❌ "Constructor has void return type."

✅ "Constructor has no return type, not even void."


--------------------------------------------------

❌ "Default constructor and no-argument constructor are always
the same."

✅ Default constructor → compiler-provided when no constructor
   is declared.

✅ No-argument constructor → any constructor with zero parameters.


--------------------------------------------------

❌ "Constructor can be overridden."

✅ Constructors cannot be overridden.


======================================================================
QUICK REVISION
----------------------------------------------------------------------

CONSTRUCTOR

Same class name
      ↓
No return type
      ↓
Called during object creation
      ↓
Initializes object


TYPES

No-Argument
      +
Parameterized
      +
Default (compiler-provided)


CONSTRUCTOR vs METHOD

Constructor
→ Initializes object

Method
→ Performs behavior/operation


MEMORY TRICK

Constructor = CREATE + INITIALIZE

Method = PERFORM + REUSE


======================================================================
