======================================================================
1. DOES JAVA SUPPORT OPERATOR OVERLOADING?
----------------------------------------------------------------------

Interview Answer

Java does NOT support user-defined operator overloading.

Operator overloading means giving an operator such as:

    +   -   *   /

different meanings or behaviors depending on the operands.

Java intentionally does not allow developers to define their own
behavior for operators because it can make code:

• Complex
• Ambiguous
• Difficult to understand
• Difficult to maintain


======================================================================
2. EXAMPLE OF OPERATOR OVERLOADING
----------------------------------------------------------------------

In languages such as C++, developers can define how operators
behave for their own classes.

Java does not allow this kind of user-defined operator
overloading.


======================================================================
3. IMPORTANT JAVA EXCEPTION
----------------------------------------------------------------------

Although Java does not support USER-DEFINED operator overloading,
some operators already have predefined/built-in behavior.

Example:

int result = 10 + 20;

Here:

+ → Numeric addition


But:

String result = "Hello " + "Java";

Here:

+ → String concatenation


So Java itself provides predefined behavior for `+`.

IMPORTANT:

❌ "Java doesn't support operator overloading at all."

Better:

✅ "Java does not support USER-DEFINED operator overloading."


======================================================================
4. WHY DOES JAVA NOT SUPPORT USER-DEFINED OPERATOR OVERLOADING?
----------------------------------------------------------------------

Main reasons:

1. Simplicity
   → Keeps Java syntax simple.

2. Readability
   → Operators have predictable meanings.

3. Avoids Ambiguity
   → Prevents developers from assigning unexpected meanings
     to operators.

4. Maintainability
   → Code becomes easier to understand and maintain.


======================================================================
5. JAVA vs C++
----------------------------------------------------------------------

+--------------------------+-------------------------+
| Java                     | C++                     |
+--------------------------+-------------------------+
| No user-defined operator | Supports user-defined  |
| overloading              | operator overloading   |
+--------------------------+-------------------------+
| Simpler operator rules   | More flexible          |
+--------------------------+-------------------------+
| + has predefined         | Operators can be       |
| behavior                 | overloaded by classes  |
+--------------------------+-------------------------+


======================================================================
COMMON INTERVIEW TRAPS
----------------------------------------------------------------------

❌ "Java does not support operator overloading."

This is incomplete.

✅ "Java does not support USER-DEFINED operator overloading."


--------------------------------------------------

❌ "`+` only performs addition in Java."

✅ `+` can perform numeric addition and String concatenation.


--------------------------------------------------

❌ "Operator overloading and method overloading are the same."

✅ They are different concepts.

Method Overloading
→ Same method name + different parameter lists.

Operator Overloading
→ Same operator + different behavior depending on operands.


======================================================================
QUICK REVISION
----------------------------------------------------------------------

Java
  ↓
No USER-DEFINED operator overloading
  ↓
Why?
  ↓
Simplicity + Readability + Avoid Ambiguity


But:

10 + 20
  ↓
Addition

"Hello " + "Java"
  ↓
String Concatenation


