Q72. ARRAYLIST vs LINKEDLIST
----------------------------------------------------------------------

ArrayList:

→ Internally uses a dynamically resizable array.

LinkedList:

→ Uses a doubly linked list.
→ Each node contains data and references to neighboring nodes.


Both:

✓ Maintain insertion order
✓ Allow duplicates
✓ Implement List
✓ Support index-based access


Performance:

+-------------------------+------------+------------+
| Operation               | ArrayList  | LinkedList |
+-------------------------+------------+------------+
| get(index)              | O(1)       | O(n)       |
| set(index, value)       | O(1)       | O(n)       |
| add(element) at end     | O(1)*      | O(1)       |
| insert at middle        | O(n)       | O(n)**     |
| remove at middle        | O(n)       | O(n)**     |
+-------------------------+------------+------------+

* Amortized O(1)

** Finding the position/node can take O(n). Once the node is
   already known, LinkedList insertion/removal can be O(1).


WHY IS ARRAYLIST GET O(1)?

ArrayList uses an array.

Example:

list.get(3)

The required index can be directly accessed.


WHY IS LINKEDLIST GET O(n)?

LinkedList must traverse nodes until it reaches the required
position.


======================================================================
WHEN TO PREFER WHICH?
----------------------------------------------------------------------

Choose ArrayList when:

✓ Frequent random/index-based access is required
✓ Reads are more frequent
✓ Insertions/deletions in the middle are relatively uncommon


Choose LinkedList when:

✓ Frequent insertion/deletion at the beginning/end is required
✓ Random access is not a major requirement


Memory Trick:

ArrayList
    ↓
FAST ACCESS


LinkedList
    ↓
FAST INSERT/DELETE AT ENDS


======================================================================
COMMON INTERVIEW TRAPS
----------------------------------------------------------------------

❌ "ArrayList is fixed size."

✅ ArrayList is dynamically resizable.


❌ "LinkedList always has O(1) insertion."

✅ Insertion can be O(1) after reaching/knowing the required node.
   Finding the position can take O(n).


❌ "LinkedList is always better for insertion/deletion."

✅ It depends on where the operation occurs and whether the node
   is already known.


❌ "Map extends Collection."

✅ Map is a separate hierarchy.


❌ "Collection and Collections are the same."

✅ Collection → Interface
   Collections → Utility class


❌ "ArrayList and LinkedList are interfaces."

✅ Both are concrete implementations of the List interface.


