Q76. HASHSET vs TREESET
----------------------------------------------------------------------

Interview Answer:

HashSet and TreeSet both implement the Set interface and do not
allow duplicate elements.

The main difference is ordering and performance.

+-------------------+---------------------+----------------------+
| Feature           | HashSet             | TreeSet              |
+-------------------+---------------------+----------------------+
| Duplicate         | Not allowed         | Not allowed          |
| Ordering          | No guaranteed order | Sorted order         |
| Internal basis    | HashMap             | Tree-based structure |
| Basic operations  | O(1) average        | O(log n)             |
| Null              | Allows one null     | Generally no null    |
| Main use          | Fast lookup         | Sorted unique data   |
+-------------------+---------------------+----------------------+


HashSet:

Set<Integer> set = new HashSet<>();

set.add(30);
set.add(10);
set.add(20);


No guaranteed sorted order.


TreeSet:

Set<Integer> set = new TreeSet<>();

set.add(30);
set.add(10);
set.add(20);


Result:

[10, 20, 30]


Memory Trick:

HashSet
→ Fast + Unordered


TreeSet
→ Sorted + O(log n)


