Q80. HASHMAP vs HASHTABLE
----------------------------------------------------------------------

Interview Answer:

HashMap and Hashtable both store key-value pairs, but HashMap is
generally preferred in modern Java.

HashMap is not synchronized and allows one null key and multiple
null values.

Hashtable is synchronized and does not allow null keys or null
values.


+-------------------+----------------------+----------------------+
| Feature           | HashMap              | Hashtable            |
+-------------------+----------------------+----------------------+
| Synchronization   | Not synchronized     | Synchronized         |
| Null key          | One allowed          | Not allowed          |
| Null values       | Allowed              | Not allowed          |
| Performance       | Generally faster     | Generally slower     |
| Type               | Modern/common        | Legacy               |
+-------------------+----------------------+----------------------+


Memory Trick:

HashMap
→ Fast
→ Not synchronized
→ Allows null


Hashtable
→ Synchronized
→ No null
→ Legacy


IMPORTANT:

Do not say Hashtable stores "address" instead of values.

Both store:

Key → Value


Modern Java:

If concurrent access is required, ConcurrentHashMap is generally
preferred over Hashtable.


======================================================================
Q81. HASHMAP vs TREEMAP
----------------------------------------------------------------------

Interview Answer:

HashMap and TreeMap both implement the Map interface and store
key-value pairs.

HashMap does not guarantee sorted order and generally provides
O(1) average-time basic operations.

TreeMap maintains keys in sorted order and generally provides
O(log n) basic operations because it uses a Red-Black tree.


+-------------------+----------------------+----------------------+
| Feature           | HashMap              | TreeMap              |
+-------------------+----------------------+----------------------+
| Ordering          | No guaranteed order  | Sorted by keys       |
| Internal basis    | Hash table           | Red-Black tree       |
| Basic operations  | O(1) average         | O(log n)             |
| Duplicate keys    | Not allowed          | Not allowed          |
| Null key          | One allowed          | Generally not allowed|
| Main use          | Fast lookup          | Sorted key-value data|
+-------------------+----------------------+----------------------+


Memory Trick:

HashMap
→ Fast lookup
→ No sorting
→ O(1) average


TreeMap
→ Sorted keys
→ Tree structure
→ O(log n)


IMPORTANT TERM:

TreeMap is a tree-based data structure.

Do NOT say "live structure."


