Q101. WHAT IS AN EXCEPTION?
----------------------------------------------------------------------

Interview Answer:

An exception is an abnormal condition or event that occurs during
program execution and disrupts the normal flow of the program.

Example:

int result = 10 / 0;

→ ArithmeticException


Memory:

Exception
   ↓
Abnormal condition
   ↓
Normal flow interrupted
   ↓
Can be handled using exception handling


======================================================================
Q102. WHAT IS EXCEPTION HANDLING?
----------------------------------------------------------------------

Interview Answer:

Exception handling is a mechanism in Java used to handle exceptions
so that the program can respond to abnormal situations gracefully
instead of terminating unexpectedly.

Java provides:

• try
• catch
• finally
• throw
• throws


Important:

Exception handling does not mean that an exception never occurs.
It means we handle the exception appropriately.


======================================================================
Q104. EXCEPTION HIERARCHY
----------------------------------------------------------------------

                    Throwable
                   /         \
                  /           \
               Error        Exception
                             /       \
                            /         \
                Checked Exception   RuntimeException
                                      |
                              Unchecked Exceptions


Examples:

Error
├── OutOfMemoryError
└── StackOverflowError

Exception
├── IOException          → Checked
├── SQLException         → Checked
└── RuntimeException     → Unchecked
    ├── NullPointerException
    ├── ArithmeticException
    └── ArrayIndexOutOfBoundsException


Important:

Error
→ Serious JVM/system-level problem
→ Generally not handled by application code

Exception
→ Application-level abnormal condition
→ Generally can be handled


Interview Trap:

RuntimeException is itself an Exception.

Its subclasses are called unchecked exceptions because the compiler
does not force us to handle or declare them.


======================================================================
Q105. CHECKED vs UNCHECKED EXCEPTIONS
----------------------------------------------------------------------

Interview Answer:

Checked exceptions are exceptions for which the compiler requires
the programmer to handle or declare them.

Unchecked exceptions are RuntimeException subclasses for which the
compiler does not force handling or declaration.


+----------------------+-------------------------+
| Checked              | Unchecked               |
+----------------------+-------------------------+
| Compiler enforced    | Not compiler enforced   |
| Handle or declare    | No requirement          |
| IOException          | NullPointerException    |
| SQLException         | ArithmeticException     |
+----------------------+-------------------------+


Example Checked:

FileReader f = new FileReader("data.txt");

May cause FileNotFoundException.

Java requires handling or declaring it.


Example Unchecked:

int result = 10 / 0;

→ ArithmeticException

Compiler does not force us to catch it.


Memory:

CHECKED
→ Compiler checks handling
→ IOException

UNCHECKED
→ RuntimeException
→ NullPointerException
→ ArithmeticException


IMPORTANT:

The exception itself generally occurs at runtime.

For checked exceptions, the compiler checks whether the exception
has been handled or declared.


======================================================================
Q106. TRY-CATCH BLOCK
----------------------------------------------------------------------

Interview Answer:

A try-catch block is used to handle exceptions in Java.

Potentially risky code is placed inside the try block.

If an exception occurs, the remaining statements in the try block
are skipped and control transfers to the matching catch block.


Example:

try {
    int result = 10 / 0;
    System.out.println(result);
}
catch (ArithmeticException e) {
    System.out.println("Cannot divide by zero");
}


Flow:

try
 ↓
Exception occurs
 ↓
Remaining try statements skipped
 ↓
Matching catch
 ↓
Exception handled
 ↓
Program continues


Important:

The catch block should generally catch the appropriate exception
type.


======================================================================
Q107. PURPOSE OF FINALLY
----------------------------------------------------------------------

Interview Answer:

The finally block is used to execute cleanup or important code
regardless of whether an exception occurs or not.

It is commonly used for:

• Closing files
• Releasing database connections
• Releasing resources
• Cleanup operations


Example:

try {
    int result = 10 / 0;
}
catch (ArithmeticException e) {
    System.out.println("Exception handled");
}
finally {
    System.out.println("Cleanup code");
}


Flow:

try
 ↓
Exception?
 ↓
catch → Handle
 ↓
finally → Cleanup


Memory:

finally
→ Cleanup


Interview Trap:

Do not say finally is specifically for printing important messages.

Its standard purpose is resource cleanup.


Note:

"finally always runs" is a useful rule, but not an absolute guarantee
if the JVM is forcibly terminated.


======================================================================
Q108. CAN TRY EXIST WITHOUT CATCH?
----------------------------------------------------------------------

Interview Answer:

Yes, a try block can exist without a catch block, but it must be
followed by a finally block.

A try block cannot exist independently.


Valid:

try {
    System.out.println("Business logic");
}
finally {
    System.out.println("Cleanup");
}


Invalid:

try {
    System.out.println("Business logic");
}


Rule:

try
 ↓
Must have at least one:
   ├── catch
   └── finally


If an exception occurs and there is no catch, the exception is not
handled there. After finally executes, it propagates to the caller.


======================================================================
Q109. throw vs throws
----------------------------------------------------------------------

Interview Answer:

throw is used to explicitly throw an exception.

throws is used in a method declaration to declare that a method may
throw one or more exceptions.


throw:

if (age < 18) {
    throw new IllegalArgumentException("Not eligible");
}


throws:

public void readFile() throws IOException {
    // file handling
}


Comparison:

+------------------+-----------------------------+
| throw            | throws                      |
+------------------+-----------------------------+
| Actually throws  | Declares possible exception |
| Used inside code | Used in method signature    |
| Usually one      | Can declare multiple       |
| exception        | exceptions                 |
+------------------+-----------------------------+


Memory:

THROW
→ "Throw it now!"

THROWS
→ "This method may throw it."


======================================================================
Q111. CUSTOM EXCEPTION
----------------------------------------------------------------------

Interview Answer:

A custom exception is a user-defined exception created for
application-specific error conditions.

It can extend:

• Exception → Checked custom exception
• RuntimeException → Unchecked custom exception


Example:

class InvalidAgeException extends Exception {

    InvalidAgeException(String message) {
        super(message);
    }
}


Using it:

public static void checkAge(int age)
        throws InvalidAgeException {

    if (age < 18) {
        throw new InvalidAgeException(
            "Age must be 18 or above"
        );
    }
}


Understand:

extends Exception
→ Creates custom exception class

throw new InvalidAgeException(...)
→ Actually throws it

throws InvalidAgeException
→ Declares method may throw it


Memory:

CREATE
→ extends Exception / RuntimeException

THROW
→ throw new ...

DECLARE
→ throws ...


======================================================================
Q112. EXCEPTION PROPAGATION
----------------------------------------------------------------------

Interview Answer:

Exception propagation is the process by which an exception moves up
the call stack from the method where it occurs to its calling method
until it is handled by a matching catch block.


Example:

static void method3() {
    int x = 10 / 0;
}

static void method2() {
    method3();
}

static void method1() {
    method2();
}

public static void main(String[] args) {

    try {
        method1();
    }
    catch (ArithmeticException e) {
        System.out.println("Exception handled");
    }
}


Flow:

main()
  ↓
method1()
  ↓
method2()
  ↓
method3()
  ↓
Exception occurs
  ↓
No handler
  ↓
Back to method2()
  ↓
No handler
  ↓
Back to method1()
  ↓
No handler
  ↓
main()
  ↓
catch
  ↓
Handled


Memory:

Exception occurs
      ↓
Current method
      ↓
Caller
      ↓
Caller
      ↓
Matching catch
      ↓
Handled


If no matching catch is found, the exception reaches the JVM's
uncaught-exception handling mechanism and the thread terminates.


======================================================================
Q113. TRY-WITH-RESOURCES
----------------------------------------------------------------------

Interview Answer:

Try-with-resources is a Java feature that automatically closes
resources after they are used.

It is used with resources implementing:

• AutoCloseable
• Closeable


Examples:

• FileReader
• FileInputStream
• Database connections
• Streams


Example:

try (FileReader reader = new FileReader("data.txt")) {

    int data = reader.read();
    System.out.println(data);

}
catch (IOException e) {
    System.out.println("File error");
}


When the try block finishes:

→ reader is automatically closed.


Traditional approach:

try
 ↓
Use resource
 ↓
finally
 ↓
Close resource


Try-with-resources:

try
 ↓
Use resource
 ↓
Automatically close


Advantages:

• Prevents resource leaks
• Less boilerplate
• Automatic cleanup
• Safer resource management


Important:

The resource must implement AutoCloseable or Closeable.


