Q115. WHAT IS JVM?
----------------------------------------------------------------------

Interview Answer:

JVM stands for Java Virtual Machine.

It is the runtime environment that executes Java bytecode.

The Java compiler converts source code into bytecode, and the JVM
loads, verifies, and executes that bytecode.

Flow:

Java Source Code (.java)
        ↓
      javac
        ↓
Bytecode (.class)
        ↓
       JVM
        ↓
Load + Verify + Execute
        ↓
Machine/Native Instructions


IMPORTANT:

javac → Source Code → Bytecode

JVM → Bytecode → Execution


JVM responsibilities include:

• Class Loading
• Bytecode Verification
• Bytecode Execution
• Memory Management
• Garbage Collection


Why Java is platform independent:

The same bytecode can run on different operating systems through
their respective JVM implementations.


======================================================================
Q116. RESPONSIBILITIES OF JVM
----------------------------------------------------------------------

Interview Answer:

The main responsibilities of JVM are class loading, bytecode
verification, bytecode execution, memory management, and garbage
collection.


Main responsibilities:

1. Class Loading
   → Loads .class files into JVM.

2. Bytecode Verification
   → Checks bytecode for safety and validity.

3. Bytecode Execution
   → Executes Java bytecode.

4. Memory Management
   → Manages runtime memory areas.

5. Garbage Collection
   → Reclaims memory occupied by unreachable objects.


Memory:

JVM
 |
 +-- Class Loading
 +-- Bytecode Verification
 +-- Execution
 +-- Memory Management
 +-- Garbage Collection


======================================================================
Q117. WHAT IS JAVA MEMORY MODEL (JMM)?
----------------------------------------------------------------------

Interview Answer:

The Java Memory Model defines how threads interact with memory.

It specifies rules related to:

• Visibility
• Ordering
• Atomicity
• Happens-before relationships


Conceptually:

                 JVM Memory
                     |
          +----------+----------+
          |                     |
       Thread 1              Thread 2
          |                     |
      Local data             Local data
          |                     |
          +---------+-----------+
                    |
              Shared Variables


Important:

JMM answers questions such as:

→ Will one thread see another thread's changes?
→ In what order are operations observed?
→ What synchronization guarantees exist?


Key concepts:

Visibility
→ Whether changes made by one thread are visible to another.


Ordering
→ How operations are ordered/observed between threads.


Atomicity
→ Whether an operation can be observed partially.


Happens-before
→ Defines guaranteed visibility and ordering relationships.


IMPORTANT:

JMM ≠ Heap/Stack memory.

Heap and Stack
→ JVM runtime memory areas

JMM
→ Rules governing thread-memory interaction


======================================================================
Q118. HEAP vs STACK MEMORY
----------------------------------------------------------------------

Interview Answer:

Heap memory is generally used for objects and their instance data.

Stack memory is used for method execution. Each thread has its own
stack containing stack frames, local variables, parameters, and
references.


Example:

Student s = new Student();


Conceptually:

STACK                     HEAP
-----                     ----
s ───────────────────→    Student object
                          age
                          name


Comparison:

+----------------------+-------------------------+
| Heap                 | Stack                   |
+----------------------+-------------------------+
| Objects generally    | Method stack frames     |
| allocated here       |                         |
| Shared among threads | Each thread has own     |
| Managed by GC        | Frames removed after    |
|                      | method returns          |
| Generally larger     | Generally smaller       |
+----------------------+-------------------------+


Memory:

HEAP
→ Objects

STACK
→ Methods + Local Variables + References


IMPORTANT INTERVIEW TRAP:

❌ Object moves to Stack when method executes.

✅ The object generally remains on Heap.
   The method execution uses a Stack frame.


======================================================================
Q119. METHOD AREA / METASPACE
----------------------------------------------------------------------

Interview Answer:

The Method Area is a JVM runtime memory area that stores class-level
information such as class metadata, method information, runtime
constant pools, and other class-related information.

In Java 8+, the JVM uses Metaspace for class metadata instead of the
old PermGen approach.


Before Java 8:

Method Area
    ↓
PermGen


Java 8+:

Method Area
    ↓
Metaspace
    ↓
Native Memory


Conceptually:

Method Area / Metaspace
        |
        +-- Class metadata
        +-- Method information
        +-- Runtime constant pool
        +-- Class-level information


Memory:

Heap
→ Objects

Stack
→ Method execution + local variables

Method Area / Metaspace
→ Class-level metadata


IMPORTANT:

Metaspace replaced PermGen in Java 8.


======================================================================
Q120. WHERE ARE OBJECTS STORED?
----------------------------------------------------------------------

Interview Answer:

Objects in Java are generally allocated in Heap memory.

Example:

Student s = new Student();


Conceptually:

STACK                     HEAP
-----                     ----
s ───────────────────→    Student object


Here:

Student object
→ Heap

Local reference variable s
→ Stack


IMPORTANT:

The exact physical placement may be optimized by the JVM/JIT, but
the standard interview-level model is:

Heap → Objects
Stack → Local references / method execution


======================================================================
Q121. WHAT IS GARBAGE COLLECTION?
----------------------------------------------------------------------

Interview Answer:

Garbage Collection is an automatic memory-management mechanism
provided by the JVM.

It identifies objects that are no longer reachable by the application
and reclaims the heap memory occupied by those objects.


Example:

Student s = new Student();

s = null;


Conceptually:

Before:

s ─────→ Student Object


After:

s ─────→ null

Student Object
      ↓
No longer reachable
      ↓
Eligible for Garbage Collection


IMPORTANT:

Eligible for GC ≠ immediately collected.

The JVM decides when/how garbage collection occurs.


Memory:

Object
  ↓
No reachable reference
  ↓
Garbage
  ↓
Eligible for GC
  ↓
Heap memory can be reclaimed


======================================================================
Q122. HOW DOES GARBAGE COLLECTION WORK?
----------------------------------------------------------------------

Interview Answer:

Garbage Collection works by identifying objects in the heap that are
no longer reachable from active references or GC Roots.

The garbage collector then reclaims memory occupied by unreachable
objects so that the memory can be reused.


Basic flow:

Objects created
      ↓
Stored in Heap
      ↓
Application uses objects
      ↓
Object becomes unreachable
      ↓
GC identifies unreachable object
      ↓
Memory reclaimed
      ↓
Memory available again


GC Roots can include references associated with:

• Active threads
• Stack references
• Static/class-related references
• Other JVM roots


Conceptually:

GC Roots
   ↓
Reachable Object
   ↓
Reachable Object

Unreachable Object
       ↓
    Garbage
       ↓
Memory reclaimed


IMPORTANT:

System.gc();

does NOT guarantee immediate garbage collection.

It only requests/suggests that the JVM perform GC.


Memory:

Reachable
→ Keep

Unreachable
→ Eligible for GC

GC
→ Reclaim memory


======================================================================
Q124. DIFFERENT GARBAGE COLLECTORS
----------------------------------------------------------------------

Interview Answer:

Java provides different garbage collectors optimized for different
requirements such as throughput, latency, and heap size.

Common collectors include:

• Serial GC
• Parallel GC
• G1 GC
• ZGC
• Shenandoah GC


1. SERIAL GC

→ Uses a single GC thread.
→ Suitable for smaller/simple applications.


2. PARALLEL GC

→ Uses multiple GC threads.
→ Focuses on high throughput.


3. G1 GC

G1 = Garbage First

→ Divides the heap into regions.
→ Designed for predictable pause times.
→ General-purpose collector.


4. ZGC

→ Designed for very low pause times.
→ Suitable for large heaps and latency-sensitive applications.


5. SHENANDOAH GC

→ Low-pause collector.
→ Performs much of its work concurrently.


Memory:

Serial
→ Simple

Parallel
→ Throughput

G1
→ General purpose + predictable pauses

ZGC
→ Very low latency

Shenandoah
→ Very low latency


IMPORTANT INTERVIEW TRAP:

❌ G1 is always the best garbage collector.

✅ The appropriate GC depends on application requirements, heap size,
   latency requirements, and throughput requirements.


