Q133. WHAT IS SYNCHRONIZATION?
----------------------------------------------------------------------

Interview Answer:

Synchronization is a mechanism used to control access to shared
resources when multiple threads execute concurrently.

It prevents multiple threads from modifying shared data at the same
time and helps avoid race conditions and inconsistent results.


Example:

Suppose:

Balance = ₹1000

Thread 1 → Withdraw ₹700
Thread 2 → Withdraw ₹700


Without synchronization, both threads may access and modify the
balance concurrently, resulting in an incorrect result.


Synchronization provides controlled access:

Thread 1
   ↓
[ Critical Section ]
   ↓
Thread 1 finishes
   ↓
Thread 2
   ↓
[ Critical Section ]


Java provides the synchronized keyword:

synchronized void withdraw(int amount) {
    balance = balance - amount;
}


Important:

Synchronization does NOT mean that multiple threads cannot access
the resource at all.

It controls access to the critical section, typically allowing
one thread at a time for the same object monitor.


======================================================================
Q134. EXPLAIN SYNCHRONIZATION WITH EXAMPLE
----------------------------------------------------------------------

Interview Answer:

Synchronization can be implemented using the synchronized keyword.

It allows only one thread at a time to execute a synchronized method
or block for the same object monitor.

Example:

class Counter {

    private int count = 0;

    synchronized void increment() {
        count++;
    }
}


Suppose two threads call increment():

Thread 1 → increment()
              ↓
          Lock acquired
              ↓
            count++
              ↓
          Lock released

Thread 2 → increment()
              ↓
          Lock acquired
              ↓
            count++
              ↓
          Lock released


Without synchronization:

Thread 1 → Read count
Thread 2 → Read count
Thread 1 → Update
Thread 2 → Update

        ↓
Race Condition
        ↓
Incorrect result


With synchronization:

Thread 1 → Read → Update → Finish
                         ↓
Thread 2 → Read → Update → Finish


Memory:

synchronized
     ↓
Lock
     ↓
One thread at a time
     ↓
Protect shared data
     ↓
Avoid race condition


