Q135. WHAT PROBLEMS DOES SYNCHRONIZATION SOLVE?
----------------------------------------------------------------------

Interview Answer:

Synchronization prevents race conditions and protects shared
resources from concurrent modification.

It helps maintain data consistency by controlling access to
critical sections.


Problems addressed:

• Race conditions
• Inconsistent data
• Concurrent modification of shared resources
• Unsafe access to critical sections


Example:

Without synchronization:

Thread 1 ──┐
            ├──→ Shared Data
Thread 2 ──┘
                 ↓
          Race Condition
                 ↓
          Wrong/Inconsistent Result


With synchronization:

Thread 1 → Lock → Critical Section → Unlock
                                      ↓
Thread 2 → Lock → Critical Section → Unlock


Memory:

Synchronization
→ Shared Resource
→ Controlled Access
→ Prevent Race Condition
→ Consistent Data


======================================================================
IMPORTANT INTERVIEW TRAPS
----------------------------------------------------------------------

1. ❌ Synchronization means threads execute one by one everywhere.

   ✅ Synchronization controls access to shared resources/critical
      sections.


2. ❌ Synchronization prevents multiple threads from accessing a
      resource.

   ✅ It controls how threads access the shared resource.


3. ❌ Concurrent execution always means parallel execution.

   ✅ Concurrency can occur through context switching on one core.
      Parallelism can occur on multiple cores.


4. ❌ Threads have separate Heap memory.

   ✅ Threads share the process Heap but have their own Stack.


5. ❌ synchronized makes the whole application single-threaded.

   ✅ Only the synchronized method/block protected by the same
      monitor is restricted.


